namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup creation

\section creation-intro Introduction

There are many ways to create arrays:

- From default values;
- Row by row, copying C arrays or ranges;
- By wrapping a data pointer;
- Through builtin functions like `fill()` and `linspace()`;
- Through generators with `generate()`;
- As the result of a transfrom: arithmetic operation, mathematical function, filter...
- As shallow or deep copies, or as slices of other arrays.

\section creation-default Default

Basic `Sequence` and `Image` constructors default-initialize the elements.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp default

\section creation-rowwise From C arrays and ranges

Sequences can be created from any array-like object, including C arrays, using either the constructor or the `resize()` function.
The latter accepts a template parameter specifying the memory space of the sequence, defaulting to the most accelerated device.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp resize

Image of rank up to 3 can be created from brace-enclosed lists or C arrays representing rows using function `rowwise()`.
The data type is either explicit or deduced from the input data type.
Like `resize()`, `rowwise()` accepts a template parameter specifying the memory space of the image.


\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp rowwise

\section creation-wrap Pointer wrapping

`Sequence` and `Image` can serve as adaptors for C pointers, in which case they do not manage memory:
no allocation or deallocation is performed, and the pointer must remain valid throughout the lifetime of the container.
This is especially useful to enable signal or image processing for arbitrary containers.
`Raster` is a row-major ordered image with contiguous memory and compatible with the standard ranges concepts.
As such, it is particularly well suited to interface with the standard library.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp wrap

\section creation-builtins Builtin functions

Many functions are provided to create sequences and images from few parameters.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp builtins

\section creation-generators Generators

Arrays can be created from generators (functions, lambdas, or functors) using function `generate()`.

If the generator depends on the position, the dimensions of the arrays have to be specified.
In this case, the generator is called with the sequence of indices as arguments.
A special case is the use of random number generators, which generally return values independent of the indices.

If the generator depends on other arrays, the dimensions are deduced from the first array passed as argument.
In this case, the generator is called with one element of each array at a time, while looping over the domain of the first array.

Note the use of `KOKKOS_LAMBDA` instead of `[=]` to declare lambdas, to ensure portability.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp generators

\section creation-result As the result of an operation

Arrays can also be created as the result of an operation on other arrays.
These operations are described in \ref pixelwise and \ref filtering.

Below is a teaser!
For more details, refer to the dedicated pages.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp result

\see \ref pixelwise
\see \ref filtering

\section creation-views Shallow and deep copies, slicing

Arrays can be returned by deep or shallow copy.
Shallow copies are the default behavior in Linx.
This means that several copies share the same data, and modifying one copy modifies all copies.
In contrast, deep copy has to be requested explicitely, either with prefix operator `+` or copy methods.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp copy

In addition to full-domain copies, `Sequence` and `Image` support slicing,
another shallow copy mechanism in which only a subset of the original array is returned.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp slicing

\see \ref regions

*/

}
